1
00:00:12,179 --> 00:00:16,140
Final Fantasy VIII is not my favorite Final Fantasy game.

2
00:00:16,940 --> 00:00:19,539
That honor belongs to Final Fantasy Tactics.

3
00:00:20,100 --> 00:00:25,420
However, Final Fantasy VIII might still be the most important game in the franchise to me.

4
00:00:26,359 --> 00:00:32,380
I imagine my feelings towards this game are somewhat similar to the way that a lot of Final Fantasy fans feel about VII.

5
00:00:33,719 --> 00:00:39,979
For many, Final Fantasy VII was not only their introduction to the franchise, but to Japanese RPGs as a whole.

6
00:00:40,420 --> 00:00:45,380
It was incredibly popular, and it pushed the boundaries with its stunning CG cutscenes.

7
00:00:46,619 --> 00:00:51,359
The systems of the game were very accessible, fun, and they encouraged experimentation,

8
00:00:52,140 --> 00:00:56,100
and the industrial setting was also perfectly tailored to a more mature audience,

9
00:00:56,479 --> 00:01:00,320
and played around with many elements that the genre wasn't known for at the time.

10
00:01:01,460 --> 00:01:04,659
It's no wonder that the game has the legacy that it does,

11
00:01:05,140 --> 00:01:10,239
and why Square to this day are still desperately attempting to recapture that lightning in a bottle.

12
00:01:11,400 --> 00:01:15,140
However, Final Fantasy VII was not my first Final Fantasy.

13
00:01:16,180 --> 00:01:21,879
I was introduced to the franchise when I walked into my basement one fateful day and saw my brother and

14
00:01:21,879 --> 00:01:27,640
his friend playing through what I now assumed to be a burned copy of the Final Fantasy VIII Pizza Hut

15
00:01:27,640 --> 00:01:34,420
demo disc, which featured a kick-ass MIDI song that I and many others would go on to use in their Neopets shop.

16
00:01:39,329 --> 00:01:43,599
For those who don't know, Final Fantasy VIII is a very divisive game.

17
00:01:44,420 --> 00:01:48,260
It had the undesirable task of trying to follow up Final Fantasy VII,

18
00:01:48,980 --> 00:01:51,659
and instead of playing it safe when developing the sequel,

19
00:01:52,219 --> 00:01:57,439
Square decided now was the time to try and experiment with many of the core elements of the genre,

20
00:01:58,019 --> 00:02:01,299
as well as to push forward with some of the concepts that made VII so good,

21
00:02:01,780 --> 00:02:04,519
emphasizing the CG cutscenes again, and trying to get to the game.

22
00:02:04,560 --> 00:02:08,479
Trimming away even more fantasy elements to make way for a modern aesthetic.

23
00:02:09,680 --> 00:02:14,259
As you might know or have guessed, Final Fantasy VIII was not lightning in a bottle.

24
00:02:15,180 --> 00:02:17,539
It's a very strange and flawed game,

25
00:02:18,400 --> 00:02:22,580
and it's one of the only JRPGs I still play through to completion fairly regularly.

26
00:02:23,400 --> 00:02:28,000
I had to overwrite numerous save files on the Steam version just to get footage for this video.

27
00:02:29,280 --> 00:02:30,900
So let's talk about Final Fantasy VIII.

28
00:02:31,659 --> 00:02:36,419
I'll first try to summarize the major criticisms that most people have with this game,

29
00:02:36,840 --> 00:02:42,280
and after that I'll dive deeper into the parts of the game that I really enjoy, despite all the mess.

30
00:02:44,340 --> 00:02:45,719
To start getting into those flaws,

31
00:02:45,939 --> 00:02:51,419
allow me to set the scene for you the experience of someone playing this game for the first time around

32
00:02:51,419 --> 00:02:52,400
the time that it was released.

33
00:02:54,639 --> 00:02:57,300
Upon starting a new save of Final Fantasy VIII,

34
00:02:57,719 --> 00:03:04,539
you are first shown this beautifully rendered cutscene unlike anything you've ever seen before, complete with a choir and orchestra,

35
00:03:05,139 --> 00:03:08,060
and a lot of strange imagery that doesn't really mean too much yet.

36
00:03:08,860 --> 00:03:13,900
It only really serves to get your brain juices flowing about what sort of game it is that you are about to play.

37
00:03:14,659 --> 00:03:18,979
And it's not even clear until afterwards that at least some of what you saw may have actually happened,

38
00:03:19,620 --> 00:03:24,979
and is likely the reason why Squall, or whatever you named your main character, ended up in this infirmary.

39
00:03:25,900 --> 00:03:30,180
As for the rest of what you saw, you are going to be waiting a long time for those answers.

40
00:03:31,659 --> 00:03:34,979
A mysterious girl calls you by your name before disappearing,

41
00:03:35,500 --> 00:03:39,620
only to be replaced by a second girl whom the player is also meeting for the first time.

42
00:03:40,520 --> 00:03:43,199
After a slightly overwhelming dump of exposition,

43
00:03:43,800 --> 00:03:48,180
you learn that you are essentially a cadet in some kind of military academy.

44
00:03:49,139 --> 00:03:55,439
Your teacher, Quistus, is here to remind you that you have still to complete your mandatory homework of enslaving a

45
00:03:55,439 --> 00:03:59,199
local deity, or guardian force to use the proper terminology.

46
00:04:00,800 --> 00:04:04,719
She offers to come with you to Ifrit's Cavern so that you can quickly finish this up,

47
00:04:05,020 --> 00:04:07,439
and be allowed to go on the class field trip.

48
00:04:08,120 --> 00:04:11,259
Spoiler, the field trip is Juno Beach on D-Day.

49
00:04:11,840 --> 00:04:12,280
Buckle up.

50
00:04:13,439 --> 00:04:17,600
So you settled on this quest, and right as you are about to step into the field,

51
00:04:18,160 --> 00:04:21,040
you instead encounter the first major problem of this game.

52
00:04:22,339 --> 00:04:27,639
Before you have even had a chance to experience combat, you are instead given the first of many,

53
00:04:27,899 --> 00:04:32,720
many tutorials about the new system that Final Fantasy VIII uses, the Junction System.

54
00:04:34,040 --> 00:04:37,079
We are roughly 15 minutes into a new game,

55
00:04:37,439 --> 00:04:41,560
and most of the game's major criticisms stem from this right here.

56
00:04:41,899 --> 00:04:44,720
The Junction System is, in reality,

57
00:04:45,019 --> 00:04:51,019
a multitude of smaller systems all working in tandem to function as Final Fantasy VIII's build customization

58
00:04:51,360 --> 00:04:52,560
and progression system.

59
00:04:53,459 --> 00:04:58,879
The game's method for dealing with magic, summons, and other combat actions are also married to this system,

60
00:04:59,620 --> 00:05:05,519
although that won't become clear on your first playthrough until you finally get a chance to experiment with it on your own.

61
00:05:06,319 --> 00:05:12,699
However, Final Fantasy VIII is intent to not let you do anything other than sift through menus until it is

62
00:05:12,699 --> 00:05:15,819
utterly convinced that you can pass an exam on the Junction System.

63
00:05:17,220 --> 00:05:22,240
If the developers were looking to make you feel like a problem student who needed his teacher to come help

64
00:05:22,240 --> 00:05:24,660
him after class, then the game has succeeded.

65
00:05:26,120 --> 00:05:31,139
I imagine this is where Final Fantasy VIII lost or confused most of its returning fanbase.

66
00:05:31,779 --> 00:05:37,420
Because if you aren't utterly determined to sit down and learn all of these menus and all of this new

67
00:05:37,420 --> 00:05:39,079
terminology right from the get-go,

68
00:05:39,620 --> 00:05:43,240
then you are likely just going to skip through these tutorials as fast as possible,

69
00:05:43,779 --> 00:05:45,920
just so you can actually see what the game feels like for you.

70
00:05:47,220 --> 00:05:53,759
I think most players would have been content if Quistis telling us how to use the gunblade was the first

71
00:05:53,759 --> 00:05:55,579
thing that the game attempted to teach us.

72
00:05:56,019 --> 00:06:03,500
But holy hell, the amount and length of these tutorials in the beginning section of Final Fantasy VIII is utterly daunting.

73
00:06:05,060 --> 00:06:09,879
The game does later attempt to have characters teach us small things in battle, which is great,

74
00:06:10,759 --> 00:06:16,779
but for the most part, it's going to be throwing terminology, abbreviations, menus, and submenus at you.

75
00:06:17,819 --> 00:06:23,500
The pacing here is what makes the Junction System feel a lot more difficult than it actually is.

76
00:06:24,100 --> 00:06:30,079
What they probably should have done was just give Squall and Quistis a basic set of junctions to start,

77
00:06:30,660 --> 00:06:33,519
and then after you obtain Ifrit from the Fire Cavern,

78
00:06:33,959 --> 00:06:38,339
explain how to Junction him and some magic onto your newly acquired Strength stat.

79
00:06:38,980 --> 00:06:39,800
And that's it.

80
00:06:40,500 --> 00:06:45,740
Have some faith that your players can understand what's actually going on here and clean up those damn menus.

81
00:06:47,500 --> 00:06:53,399
So, other than the bloated tutorials, what are the actual problems of the Junction System?

82
00:06:53,819 --> 00:06:58,459
Well, the problems lay in its differences from traditional RPG systems,

83
00:06:58,879 --> 00:07:02,540
and the first of those differences is how the game treats magic.

84
00:07:03,779 --> 00:07:09,160
Unlike in Final Fantasy VI or VII, you do not acquire new magic through espers or materia.

85
00:07:09,939 --> 00:07:13,639
It is instead drawn directly from enemies, or from draw points.

86
00:07:14,579 --> 00:07:19,600
It is treated almost like a consumable item, no mana or MP required.

87
00:07:20,120 --> 00:07:25,100
Alternatively, you can cast magic directly from an enemy instead of stalking it beforehand,

88
00:07:25,560 --> 00:07:27,579
which potentially saves you a turn in combat.

89
00:07:28,420 --> 00:07:35,259
However, magic that you store on your person can be junctioned to a specific stat to increase it,

90
00:07:35,680 --> 00:07:39,759
essentially allowing you to build your party with whatever archetypes that you had in mind.

91
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:44,540
The problem comes with how incredibly tedious this is.

92
00:07:45,339 --> 00:07:48,800
Drawing magic from an enemy takes a very, very long time,

93
00:07:49,060 --> 00:07:52,019
as it has to stalk all the way up to 100 for full effect.

94
00:07:53,220 --> 00:07:55,220
When junctioning different magic to a stat,

95
00:07:55,959 --> 00:07:59,819
you have no idea how effective it's going to be until you simply try it,

96
00:08:00,279 --> 00:08:03,500
as not all magic is weighted equally depending on the stat.

97
00:08:04,399 --> 00:08:08,839
For instance, cure magic is best junction to HP or vitality,

98
00:08:09,399 --> 00:08:14,800
and certain offensive magic seems to have arbitrarily better values when used on strength.

99
00:08:16,100 --> 00:08:23,680
This creates a problem where the excitement for discovering new unidentified magic on an enemy is quickly replaced by the

100
00:08:23,680 --> 00:08:27,920
tedium of drawing it out over many, many turns into a stack of 100,

101
00:08:28,740 --> 00:08:33,320
only then to potentially realize that it is no better than any of your current junctions.

102
00:08:34,919 --> 00:08:38,799
Junctioning magic also creates a dubious little mind goblin for many players,

103
00:08:39,559 --> 00:08:44,639
as casting magic that is junctioned to a stat obviously makes that stat decrease over time,

104
00:08:45,100 --> 00:08:48,179
as well as diminishing that neat stack of 100 that you saved up.

105
00:08:49,299 --> 00:08:53,220
Some players will deal with this by simply not casting magic at all,

106
00:08:53,700 --> 00:08:58,440
unless it is cast from an enemy or can be easily replaced and therefore not missed.

107
00:08:59,639 --> 00:09:04,279
This all sounds pretty bad, but the system was designed for a reason.

108
00:09:05,039 --> 00:09:10,620
It gives the players a way to improve and customize their party members without actually having to grind for levels.

109
00:09:11,720 --> 00:09:16,320
Final Fantasy VIII veterans know that the best way to get magic is not through combat,

110
00:09:16,860 --> 00:09:18,740
but by refining items and cards into magic.

111
00:09:19,840 --> 00:09:23,639
When you figure this out, then the game becomes fairly easy,

112
00:09:24,200 --> 00:09:27,940
and that is due to a third problem, which is how the game handles leveling up.

113
00:09:29,899 --> 00:09:32,840
Leveling up in Final Fantasy VIII is a double-edged sword.

114
00:09:34,120 --> 00:09:39,080
This is because when you level up in this game, the enemies level up alongside you.

115
00:09:39,919 --> 00:09:40,860
You heard that correctly.

116
00:09:41,580 --> 00:09:44,559
Almost every enemy in this game scales to your level.

117
00:09:45,559 --> 00:09:50,240
On paper, this system was implemented to eliminate the need for grinding.

118
00:09:51,059 --> 00:09:52,539
What a wonderful concept.

119
00:09:53,120 --> 00:09:58,220
What it actually did was create two different kinds of playthroughs of Final Fantasy VIII.

120
00:09:59,179 --> 00:10:01,179
There is your standard first-time playthrough,

121
00:10:01,720 --> 00:10:06,019
where you enter into the game not really knowing what's going on with all these new systems,

122
00:10:06,799 --> 00:10:13,340
leveling up either on purpose or on accident, whereby within a few hours you will hit a brick wall.

123
00:10:14,240 --> 00:10:19,659
The brick wall in this case is a boss, who is simply too difficult for you to conceivably defeat,

124
00:10:20,120 --> 00:10:24,340
as you have either neglected your junctions or have simply leveled up far too much.

125
00:10:25,820 --> 00:10:31,460
The second playthrough is the veterans playthrough, where you now fully understand the game's systems,

126
00:10:32,059 --> 00:10:38,179
realize that leveling up is the enemy, and will instead choose to empower yourself through the junction system,

127
00:10:38,700 --> 00:10:46,179
turning cards into items, items into magic, and junctioning that magic to become completely unstoppable, trivializing the difficulty.

128
00:10:47,600 --> 00:10:54,519
I think it is pretty clear that neither of these two were the intended outcomes of implementing this scaling leveling idea.

129
00:10:55,379 --> 00:10:58,340
It becomes a problem to be avoided or ignored.

130
00:10:58,639 --> 00:11:00,340
It actually doesn't solve anything.

131
00:11:01,340 --> 00:11:07,019
All that it has done is replace level grinding with magic grinding, making the system almost redundant.

132
00:11:09,299 --> 00:11:13,559
The last problem that many people have with the game is with its story and characters.

133
00:11:14,820 --> 00:11:20,179
I cannot comment on these flaws too much, because I personally don't fully understand most of the criticisms.

134
00:11:21,179 --> 00:11:26,419
In the end, liking a story, its setting, or its characters is all a matter of taste.

135
00:11:26,840 --> 00:11:33,340
However, I can understand why certain elements of the story would fail to resonate with a wide audience,

136
00:11:33,899 --> 00:11:37,559
or even alienate people that were looking for a more straightforward narrative.

137
00:11:38,659 --> 00:11:42,179
Final Fantasy VIII's story is very weird, and notably,

138
00:11:42,879 --> 00:11:47,200
it takes an incredibly hard to describe turn at the start of disc 3,

139
00:11:47,639 --> 00:11:49,559
that was definitely going to lose some people.

140
00:11:50,519 --> 00:11:54,840
For those who didn't finish the game the whole way through, you straight up go to space at one point.

141
00:11:55,580 --> 00:11:59,779
And there is also a time paradox which the entire story hinges upon,

142
00:12:00,279 --> 00:12:02,500
as it tries to deal with themes like fate

143
00:12:02,759 --> 00:12:03,350
and destiny.

144
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:08,620
This, of course, is ignoring the all-important amnesia subplot,

145
00:12:09,019 --> 00:12:13,919
and the mystery of the random sympathetic belts of narcolepsy that is affecting your party,

146
00:12:14,399 --> 00:12:19,059
which causes them to have dreamlike visions of characters that are far cooler than they are.

147
00:12:19,879 --> 00:12:24,100
And all this, of course, being wrapped around what is ostensibly a love story.

148
00:12:24,840 --> 00:12:26,779
It is the focus of the game, after all.

149
00:12:27,799 --> 00:12:32,600
Needless to say, this strange amalgam story was not going to be everyone's cup of tea.

150
00:12:34,179 --> 00:12:38,980
One of the most frequent criticisms you will find is with the game's main character, Squall.

151
00:12:39,899 --> 00:12:45,100
People tend to call him annoying, brooding, or emo, which is true to a degree.

152
00:12:45,799 --> 00:12:46,919
Not the emo thing, though.

153
00:12:47,100 --> 00:12:48,779
I'm not really sure where this comes from.

154
00:12:49,700 --> 00:12:50,450
Maybe it's the clothes.

155
00:12:51,700 --> 00:12:58,720
People tend to read his dialogue in a particular way, his overuse of ellipses or throwaway lines like whatever.

156
00:12:59,460 --> 00:13:01,039
By the way, that's a translation thing.

157
00:13:02,279 --> 00:13:07,000
Squall does definitely start out far more introverted and brooding than other protagonists.

158
00:13:07,679 --> 00:13:13,519
But I think that in the end, it really serves to highlight his arc, which I will cover eventually.

159
00:13:14,639 --> 00:13:18,320
The other characters also tend to get written off as being fairly unmemorable,

160
00:13:18,600 --> 00:13:21,120
but again, it really comes down to taste,

161
00:13:21,600 --> 00:13:26,759
as I think they actually get far more to do in the story than most other RPG characters of that

162
00:13:26,759 --> 00:13:29,000
era, each having their own moments.

163
00:13:29,779 --> 00:13:32,820
And seeing as how I have run out of bad things to talk about,

164
00:13:33,100 --> 00:13:35,289
I think I should begin my counterattack,

165
00:13:35,740 --> 00:13:40,000
and explain in gruesome detail why I love this horrible mess of a game.

166
00:13:42,600 --> 00:13:47,659
I will first state that my purpose here is not to try to convince you that Final Fantasy VIII is

167
00:13:47,659 --> 00:13:50,580
a good game, or that you are wrong if you dislike it.

168
00:13:51,100 --> 00:13:55,860
Instead, I simply want to highlight why many people still enjoy this game,

169
00:13:56,139 --> 00:13:59,200
and that there is some merit to a lot of the ideas that Final Fantasy VIII has.

170
00:14:00,779 --> 00:14:05,279
At best, I would encourage maybe giving the game another chance.

171
00:14:07,379 --> 00:14:09,919
Alright, let's get the easy stuff out of the way.

172
00:14:10,220 --> 00:14:13,259
The stuff that most people can agree kick ass about this game.

173
00:14:14,559 --> 00:14:19,809
If you have only played a bit of Final Fantasy VIII, or have heard anything about it through osmosis,

174
00:14:20,200 --> 00:14:24,220
then you likely know about Triple Triad, or are aware of the game's soundtrack.

175
00:14:25,740 --> 00:14:29,440
As much as I want to, I am not going to be covering Triple Triad in depth,

176
00:14:29,659 --> 00:14:33,639
as it is something I have already talked about at length in my Final Fantasy XIV video.

177
00:14:34,500 --> 00:14:40,000
It's also a bit of a running joke to refer to Final Fantasy VIII as the game with Triple Triad in it.

178
00:14:40,940 --> 00:14:42,039
For those who don't know,

179
00:14:42,379 --> 00:14:47,080
it's a fairly simple-to-learn card game that lets you collect cards either by converting enemies,

180
00:14:47,600 --> 00:14:50,700
or by playing against a myriad of NPCs within the game.

181
00:14:51,620 --> 00:14:54,200
As I hinted at before, the reward of winning is a game.

182
00:14:54,240 --> 00:14:59,600
The reward of winning cards can be used to easily empower your characters, which incentivizes you to keep playing,

183
00:14:59,919 --> 00:15:03,639
and luckily enough, and especially with the addition of optional rules,

184
00:15:04,179 --> 00:15:07,000
the game is fun enough to be worth playing in its own right.

185
00:15:07,980 --> 00:15:11,779
It is essentially the gold standard for minigames within games,

186
00:15:11,919 --> 00:15:16,000
but it's not really much of a selling point for Final Fantasy VIII on its own.

187
00:15:16,519 --> 00:15:18,240
But that song is really catchy.

188
00:15:18,840 --> 00:15:19,220
And speaking of...

189
00:15:20,899 --> 00:15:26,559
Final Fantasy VIII's soundtrack is easily a contender for the best in the franchise, excluding XIV,

190
00:15:26,759 --> 00:15:30,899
which I think has a somewhat of an unfair advantage being a long-running MMO.

191
00:15:32,059 --> 00:15:37,139
I don't think anyone is going to argue that Nobuo's music is anything other than the best in the industry.

192
00:15:37,960 --> 00:15:40,100
With Final Fantasy VIII's soundtrack in particular,

193
00:15:40,460 --> 00:15:44,200
he tried a few new things that would go on to become staples in the franchise.

194
00:15:45,480 --> 00:15:49,200
The first two songs you hear are the opening overture, followed by...

195
00:15:49,580 --> 00:15:53,039
Libri Fatale, and are an absolute knockout one to punch.

196
00:15:57,960 --> 00:16:04,399
Libri Fatale marks the first time that Nobuo would combine both choir and orchestra, which clearly paid off,

197
00:16:04,960 --> 00:16:11,039
and it's why you hear it featured so heavily in live performances for both Final Fantasy and video game concerts alike.

198
00:16:13,059 --> 00:16:16,000
The game also features the first pop song in the franchise,

199
00:16:16,360 --> 00:16:19,220
with Eyes on Me, a love song with vocals and music.

200
00:16:19,460 --> 00:16:23,820
performed by Fei Wang that was intended to take advantage of the CD technology,

201
00:16:24,259 --> 00:16:28,000
and also became the best-selling song from a video game in Japan at the time.

202
00:16:29,559 --> 00:16:33,179
Also, I'm not really sure why no one else has ever tried this before, but...

203
00:16:33,639 --> 00:16:37,139
Final Fantasy VIII also just features that song that plays outside the house?

204
00:16:41,059 --> 00:16:44,179
Seems like a strange thing for no one to have tried to capitalize on before,

205
00:16:44,399 --> 00:16:46,700
or maybe Square just has the copyright or something.

206
00:16:47,740 --> 00:16:52,460
On a personal note, Final Fantasy VIII's soundtrack is not my favorite of the franchise,

207
00:16:53,139 --> 00:16:56,139
as that honor belongs to Final Fantasy Crystal Chronicles.

208
00:16:57,139 --> 00:17:02,480
Similarly to my feelings on the game, though, it might still be the soundtrack I think about the most,

209
00:17:03,019 --> 00:17:07,539
and it plays a pretty big factor into why I get excited to pick this game up again.

210
00:17:08,920 --> 00:17:14,900
Replaying Final Fantasy VIII means looking forward to playing as Laguna, whose regular battle theme is just the best.

211
00:17:15,579 --> 00:17:16,099
Fight me in the comments.

212
00:17:17,920 --> 00:17:22,579
I also get to look forward to the various musical themes based on Succession of Witches,

213
00:17:22,700 --> 00:17:25,680
which is by far my favorite villainous theme song,

214
00:17:25,980 --> 00:17:31,680
and is tied in my head with Final Fantasy X's Hymn of the Faith as the best leitmotif of the franchise.

215
00:17:33,799 --> 00:17:35,180
To close my thoughts here,

216
00:17:35,660 --> 00:17:41,640
I mentioned in my Final Fantasy XIV video that a game only needs to have a single decent boss battle

217
00:17:41,640 --> 00:17:44,740
with a slapper of a boss track to pique my interest,

218
00:17:45,319 --> 00:17:48,700
and Final Fantasy Final Fantasy VIII has that and a ton more going on for it.

219
00:17:51,140 --> 00:17:55,099
I guess now is as good a time as any to bring up the junction system again.

220
00:17:55,579 --> 00:17:56,019
Oh boy.

221
00:17:56,660 --> 00:18:00,380
So, I have written this script a number of times now,

222
00:18:00,680 --> 00:18:07,700
and this was originally going to be a very short video centered entirely on the junction system and the merit I see in it.

223
00:18:08,359 --> 00:18:10,660
But in the process of replaying the game,

224
00:18:10,839 --> 00:18:14,480
I found myself getting a little too excited to talk about the story.

225
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:15,859
More on that later.

226
00:18:17,380 --> 00:18:23,359
To start explaining why I like the junction system, I'll need to tell you about this little guy here,

227
00:18:23,799 --> 00:18:28,799
the T-Rexar, or the much better named Archaodinos in the Japanese version.

228
00:18:29,940 --> 00:18:31,079
If you have played this game,

229
00:18:31,480 --> 00:18:36,960
you likely remember him as the big fucking dinosaur who kicked your ass in the training ground of Balamb Garden,

230
00:18:37,619 --> 00:18:40,779
or in the forest just outside if you were incredibly unlucky.

231
00:18:41,980 --> 00:18:44,640
I don't know what it is with Final Fantasy games,

232
00:18:44,859 --> 00:18:46,079
but they seem fixated on the way that I'm going to be able to do.

233
00:18:46,319 --> 00:18:50,000
This game is a good idea of sticking big ass reptiles near the start of the game.

234
00:18:50,400 --> 00:18:51,880
It's almost like a series tradition.

235
00:18:52,960 --> 00:18:57,259
Sometimes the game in question will explicitly tell you to run from these monsters,

236
00:18:57,619 --> 00:19:04,140
as fleeing from a difficult encounter is a valid option and a decent lesson to teach the player early on.

237
00:19:05,240 --> 00:19:10,380
Other games will merely imply the danger based on the placement of the monster near weaker enemies,

238
00:19:10,700 --> 00:19:13,579
and they will teach you the hard way if you try to fight them.

239
00:19:14,740 --> 00:19:20,380
These enemies almost always stick out because of how absurdly challenging they are compared to everything else you have faced

240
00:19:20,380 --> 00:19:26,039
up to that point, and this also cleverly plants the sub-goal of coming back at a later point in

241
00:19:26,039 --> 00:19:31,099
that game to finally defeat this enemy, thereby demonstrating how much stronger you have become.

242
00:19:31,940 --> 00:19:34,200
Perfect RPG gameplay, in my opinion.

243
00:19:35,519 --> 00:19:40,019
However, for more adventurous players, especially those who have played the games before,

244
00:19:40,640 --> 00:19:43,839
they may try to find a way to kill this creature early on,

245
00:19:44,359 --> 00:19:47,720
gaming the system so to speak in order to acquire early benefits,

246
00:19:48,059 --> 00:19:49,779
or simply to challenge themselves.

247
00:19:51,200 --> 00:19:54,599
This situation, however, is a little different in Final Fantasy VIII.

248
00:19:55,599 --> 00:20:01,440
When you meet with Quistis in the Balamb Garden Training Center, she directly mentions the T-Rexar,

249
00:20:01,680 --> 00:20:06,000
asking if Squall has ever fought one, which you likely haven't by this point.

250
00:20:06,960 --> 00:20:09,359
She assures you that defeating one is easy,

251
00:20:09,940 --> 00:20:13,099
as long as you are able to junction sleep magic onto your attack.

252
00:20:13,839 --> 00:20:18,430
This is obviously a new concept in this game, as it is part of the junction system,

253
00:20:19,160 --> 00:20:25,640
but the ramifications of what she mentions here won't really mean much until you finally see a T-Rexar for yourself.

254
00:20:27,259 --> 00:20:29,720
This thing is designed to kick your ass.

255
00:20:30,500 --> 00:20:35,220
Even with level scaling, the earliest version of this thing has over 10,000 health,

256
00:20:35,819 --> 00:20:37,660
and can deal hundreds of damage per hit.

257
00:20:38,380 --> 00:20:41,039
There is no way that you are killing this thing on accident.

258
00:20:41,420 --> 00:20:43,599
You need to learn how to junction to beat it.

259
00:20:44,740 --> 00:20:49,539
For comparison's sake, a Grat, the most common enemy you encounter here,

260
00:20:49,960 --> 00:20:52,720
has only about 200 health at its lowest level,

261
00:20:53,180 --> 00:20:55,140
and is lucky to hit you for 30 damage.

262
00:20:55,940 --> 00:21:01,640
However, if you are looking to find that sleep magic that Quistis mentioned, then you can draw it from them.

263
00:21:02,279 --> 00:21:02,880
How convenient.

264
00:21:04,039 --> 00:21:07,339
This tiny section in the training grounds is, in my opinion,

265
00:21:07,599 --> 00:21:10,440
the best teaching tool that Final Fantasy VIII has to offer,

266
00:21:11,299 --> 00:21:13,839
and I really wish that they made this section a little longer.

267
00:21:14,779 --> 00:21:17,180
Unlike with the previous tutorials in this game,

268
00:21:17,759 --> 00:21:23,720
here you finally get a chance to experiment on your own with an explicitly stated goal that potentially means even

269
00:21:23,720 --> 00:21:27,480
more to a player if they have become determined to kill this tough-as-hell dinosaur.

270
00:21:28,559 --> 00:21:31,059
It's not as easy as Quistis first made out either.

271
00:21:31,880 --> 00:21:35,299
Even at a 100 sleep magic junction, it can resist your attacks,

272
00:21:35,960 --> 00:21:38,559
and so you are going to want to junction your strength,

273
00:21:38,819 --> 00:21:41,140
so you can quickly work through that mountain of health.

274
00:21:41,759 --> 00:21:43,079
And if you are feeling risky,

275
00:21:43,380 --> 00:21:48,359
you might even try to take advantage of a low health squall to try and limit break as often as

276
00:21:48,359 --> 00:21:50,599
you can, making it go by even faster.

277
00:21:52,259 --> 00:21:54,680
The sense of mastery over the game's systems,

278
00:21:55,039 --> 00:21:58,880
and the satisfaction of seeing this bastard die for the first time is incredible,

279
00:21:59,839 --> 00:22:04,500
and it is one of my favorite teaching moments in any video game, hence why I bring all this up.

280
00:22:05,920 --> 00:22:08,460
This is the potential I see in the junction system.

281
00:22:09,259 --> 00:22:13,039
Not the mind goblins created by not having a perfect stack of a hundred magic,

282
00:22:13,579 --> 00:22:18,400
but a system that is just complex enough that it allows me to feel as though I can break the

283
00:22:18,400 --> 00:22:20,059
game, even if it is by design.

284
00:22:21,539 --> 00:22:23,319
I will probably die by this statement,

285
00:22:23,799 --> 00:22:28,019
but I think that Final Fantasy VIII is one of the most replayable RPGs ever made.

286
00:22:29,099 --> 00:22:30,539
Just like the T-Rex are,

287
00:22:30,700 --> 00:22:34,819
there are other moments built into the game that almost beg you to challenge yourself,

288
00:22:35,299 --> 00:22:39,859
like the huge reward you get for defeating the spider tank during the attack on Dalit City.

289
00:22:41,799 --> 00:22:45,880
As a small tradition of my own, when I go back to play Final Fantasy VIII,

290
00:22:46,339 --> 00:22:50,460
I sometimes make it my goal to kill the T-Rex R as quickly as possible,

291
00:22:51,079 --> 00:22:52,599
something like an informal speedrun.

292
00:22:53,500 --> 00:22:56,819
Usually this will take me around two hours or so from the start of the game,

293
00:22:57,180 --> 00:22:59,380
depending on how distracted I get with Triple Triad,

294
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:02,259
and it still feels satisfying for me to do,

295
00:23:02,519 --> 00:23:05,299
as I remember being intimidated by this thing when I was a kid.

296
00:23:06,180 --> 00:23:13,180
And if I'm feeling really cheeky, flashing lights warning, I'll treat myself and hit another one with an early degenerator.

297
00:23:15,339 --> 00:23:17,059
You're right, Quistus, that was easy.

298
00:23:18,720 --> 00:23:24,119
Unfortunately, there are still aspects to Final Fantasy VIII's gameplay that I cannot fully defend.

299
00:23:24,960 --> 00:23:30,119
While I don't tend to experience the same mind goblins as other people, I know why they exist.

300
00:23:31,400 --> 00:23:36,019
I didn't used to, but now I tend to use magic quite frequently during my playthroughs,

301
00:23:36,619 --> 00:23:38,000
especially in the early stages.

302
00:23:38,920 --> 00:23:43,960
It's not too difficult to acquire a huge stock of items that can be refined into more magic,

303
00:23:44,460 --> 00:23:47,200
and so I will use magic even if I have it junctioned.

304
00:23:48,299 --> 00:23:52,519
Most people, including myself on my first playthrough, do not play like this.

305
00:23:53,359 --> 00:23:56,779
Typically, players junction magic to every available stat,

306
00:23:57,019 --> 00:24:00,299
and will refuse to use magic out of fear of reducing those stats.

307
00:24:01,019 --> 00:24:05,220
And even if you did play the game like I do now, there is still a problem.

308
00:24:06,240 --> 00:24:11,000
Learning to master the junction system means learning how to avoid using the draw command.

309
00:24:12,000 --> 00:24:14,319
I almost never use the draw command,

310
00:24:14,539 --> 00:24:18,539
with the exception of the very start of the game or to draw Guardian of Forces.

311
00:24:19,559 --> 00:24:21,759
This is indicative of a large problem.

312
00:24:22,660 --> 00:24:28,259
Story-wise, your ability to draw magic is one of the most significant things that your character can do,

313
00:24:28,980 --> 00:24:31,819
and yet it is one of the most tedious things ever put into a game,

314
00:24:32,099 --> 00:24:33,720
and so you will try and avoid it.

315
00:24:34,640 --> 00:24:36,220
In the Steam port of the game,

316
00:24:36,700 --> 00:24:41,200
they decided to add the ability to straight up give yourself a stupid amount of magic,

317
00:24:41,759 --> 00:24:44,519
like a hundred of everything you can find in disc one.

318
00:24:45,079 --> 00:24:45,539
It's nuts.

319
00:24:46,440 --> 00:24:50,589
Obviously, this fucks with the progression of the game, and it's not a great solution.

320
00:24:51,220 --> 00:24:54,200
Honestly, if they wanted a one-button solution,

321
00:24:54,720 --> 00:24:58,640
I would have just added the ability to tweak how much magic that you can draw from enemies.

322
00:24:59,359 --> 00:25:00,180
An easy mode.

323
00:25:00,380 --> 00:25:00,849
I would have just added the ability to do the game, if you will,

324
00:25:01,279 --> 00:25:04,980
as it will still allow new or returning players to learn the other systems.

325
00:25:06,640 --> 00:25:11,859
I want to reiterate that the point of the junction system, as well as the scaling difficulty of the enemies,

326
00:25:12,319 --> 00:25:14,359
was to do away with traditional level grinding.

327
00:25:15,400 --> 00:25:20,180
In a very bizarre, broken, and unintended way, they almost achieved that.

328
00:25:21,420 --> 00:25:25,680
When I play the game, the only time that I grind for experience is at the very beginning,

329
00:25:26,420 --> 00:25:29,299
just long enough for Quetzalcoatl to learn card mod, and Shiva to learn how to refine the game.

330
00:25:30,380 --> 00:25:31,079
ice magic.

331
00:25:31,680 --> 00:25:36,160
I can then play as little of or as much of triple triad to refine as much magic as I

332
00:25:36,160 --> 00:25:41,440
want, depending on my goal for that playthrough, which usually takes about an hour and a half at most.

333
00:25:42,460 --> 00:25:46,450
After that, I can leisurely stroll my way through the entire story.

334
00:25:48,859 --> 00:25:56,839
One of the key features of the junction system not often talked about is the ability to freely swap all of your junctions between characters.

335
00:25:57,960 --> 00:26:05,299
This is actually a huge thing, as a large part of the story has Squall delegating the party into squads,

336
00:26:05,319 --> 00:26:06,660
which you will often switch between.

337
00:26:07,960 --> 00:26:10,480
Other Final Fantasy games sometimes attempt this,

338
00:26:10,920 --> 00:26:15,660
but it can suck with varying degrees of ass depending on how much you use those specific characters,

339
00:26:16,279 --> 00:26:19,700
sometimes leaving you with a character that you have never even used before.

340
00:26:20,759 --> 00:26:24,559
In Final Fantasy VIII, though, you can easily use every single character,

341
00:26:25,119 --> 00:26:27,799
and it allows for the story to experiment and create a new character.

342
00:26:35,220 --> 00:26:45,259
And so you can see why something like the junction system needed to exist here,

343
00:26:45,700 --> 00:26:51,339
in order to separate your character's avatar strength from leveling so that these story moments can even work in the first place.

344
00:26:52,440 --> 00:26:56,700
Also, you may now be getting hints as to why I rewrote this script so much,

345
00:26:56,859 --> 00:27:00,720
as I cannot seem to avoid getting into the story no matter what I bring up.

346
00:27:01,319 --> 00:27:03,519
So let's try to not put it off any longer,

347
00:27:03,619 --> 00:27:06,880
and get into what I really wanted to share about this game.

348
00:27:09,359 --> 00:27:12,599
I will first address the nostalgia that I have for this game,

349
00:27:12,759 --> 00:27:16,799
which probably plays the largest factor into why I replay it so often.

350
00:27:17,700 --> 00:27:22,640
It can be hard for me to separate what I genuinely enjoy about Final Fantasy VIII and its story,

351
00:27:22,779 --> 00:27:26,059
versus what I've grown accustomed to because it's what I grew up with.

352
00:27:27,160 --> 00:27:30,920
I did not play Final Fantasy VII or IX at a formative age,

353
00:27:31,319 --> 00:27:34,859
so I tend to look at those games far more critically than I do with VIII,

354
00:27:35,500 --> 00:27:40,440
and even recognizing that, it can still be difficult to tell when I am being unfair.

355
00:27:41,339 --> 00:27:43,900
In other words, this is all just my opinion,

356
00:27:44,140 --> 00:27:47,539
so please take everything that I say in this video with a pinch of salt.

357
00:27:48,059 --> 00:27:52,180
Also, it is fair to say that there will be major spoilers from here on,

358
00:27:52,460 --> 00:27:55,400
as I will be discussing certain characters and events in depth.

359
00:27:57,220 --> 00:27:59,099
Let's talk about party dynamic.

360
00:28:00,059 --> 00:28:01,660
This is a thing in RPGs,

361
00:28:02,099 --> 00:28:07,319
and it has to do with how the main characters of the game relate to each other and to the plot.

362
00:28:08,500 --> 00:28:11,579
Unlike in other RPGs, in Final Fantasy VIII,

363
00:28:11,960 --> 00:28:18,519
your party is not made up of a ragtag group of random adventurers and mascot characters with disconnected backstories.

364
00:28:18,940 --> 00:28:26,160
Instead, the bulk of your party are members of a paramilitary group called Seed, spelt with a stylized D.

365
00:28:26,839 --> 00:28:31,920
which is not actually arbitrary, but intended so that you can read it backwards as D's.

366
00:28:32,779 --> 00:28:36,299
Some of you already know what this stands for, you know if you know,

367
00:28:36,779 --> 00:28:39,440
but if you don't, feel free to ask in the comments.

368
00:28:40,779 --> 00:28:44,859
This setup is a great framing device, and it allows for you, the player,

369
00:28:45,140 --> 00:28:50,940
and Squall, the aspiring squad leader, to simultaneously meet the members of his would-be team.

370
00:28:51,900 --> 00:28:59,000
In order, you meet Quistus, who starts out as Squall's instructor and also clearly has unrequited feelings for him,

371
00:28:59,579 --> 00:29:04,940
and so agrees to step down as an instructor so that she can be assigned to his team for an upcoming mission.

372
00:29:06,019 --> 00:29:08,220
The next two, Zell and Selfie,

373
00:29:08,640 --> 00:29:13,680
are two cadets whom Squall worked with during his trial mission and graduated alongside him.

374
00:29:13,880 --> 00:29:21,660
Rinoa, the only non-Seed member, starts out as the head of a small resistance group called the Forest Owls,

375
00:29:22,039 --> 00:29:26,240
and is the client who hires Seed, thereby enlisting Squall's team.

376
00:29:27,299 --> 00:29:30,039
And the final member is another Seed Specialist,

377
00:29:30,339 --> 00:29:35,299
a sniper who is assigned to your team to carry out an assassination on a sorceress.

378
00:29:35,839 --> 00:29:36,900
More on that in a bit.

379
00:29:38,400 --> 00:29:46,420
The dynamic here does wonders to satisfy a nagging question I often find myself asking when I play games with a large cast of characters.

380
00:29:47,480 --> 00:29:50,539
That question being, why is everyone here?

381
00:29:51,680 --> 00:29:58,019
I typically ask myself this question subconsciously any time that a major plot threat is resolved in a story.

382
00:29:58,799 --> 00:30:03,079
Is there still a reason why each of the characters are invested in the main plot?

383
00:30:03,799 --> 00:30:06,859
Would it make even more sense if a character simply just left?

384
00:30:08,890 --> 00:30:14,619
A good example of a game that does not answer this question well is Final Fantasy XII.

385
00:30:15,579 --> 00:30:20,119
The bulk of the story in XII is centered around the characters of Ash and Bosh,

386
00:30:20,500 --> 00:30:22,140
who are not the main characters.

387
00:30:23,200 --> 00:30:29,440
The actual main character, Vaughn, is a fish out of water who is only circumstantially related to the other characters,

388
00:30:29,940 --> 00:30:32,180
with the exception of his friend Pinello,

389
00:30:32,579 --> 00:30:35,460
who might even have less of a reason to be in the party than him.

390
00:30:36,420 --> 00:30:42,839
The two of them are essentially strung along by a series of serendipitous events that keep them in the party

391
00:30:42,839 --> 00:30:46,039
long enough that it would simply become weird if they just left.

392
00:30:46,859 --> 00:30:49,960
And the other characters treat Vaughn almost like he's a nuisance,

393
00:30:50,599 --> 00:30:55,319
as he has to constantly ask questions about the basic history of the world on behalf of the player,

394
00:30:55,799 --> 00:30:56,680
just so that we're informed.

395
00:30:58,119 --> 00:31:00,319
Time for one of my iconic bad analogies.

396
00:31:00,940 --> 00:31:05,500
But he's like if, in Star Wars, Luke Skywalker didn't even meet Obi-Wan.

397
00:31:15,059 --> 00:31:17,160
Why is Vaughn still here?

398
00:31:18,000 --> 00:31:21,099
I guess he can swing a sword good, and he doesn't like the Empire,

399
00:31:21,599 --> 00:31:24,000
but that describes a lot of people in that setting.

400
00:31:25,079 --> 00:31:30,140
In contrast, Final Fantasy X does a great job of establishing its team dynamic.

401
00:31:31,500 --> 00:31:36,440
Once again, our main character is the Odd Man Out, an almost literally fish out of water.

402
00:31:37,339 --> 00:31:43,480
Titus' only real connection to the party is through Orin, who is also one of Yuna's father's best friends,

403
00:31:43,920 --> 00:31:45,380
and a legendary guardian.

404
00:31:46,539 --> 00:31:49,720
The rest of the party has a direct connection through Yuna,

405
00:31:50,119 --> 00:31:53,250
by being one of her guardians and helping her on her quest,

406
00:31:53,559 --> 00:31:54,619
or is her blood relative.

407
00:31:55,920 --> 00:32:00,720
The game, however, gives Titus the chance to interact with these party members on his own,

408
00:32:01,140 --> 00:32:03,079
before these other connections are revealed.

409
00:32:03,720 --> 00:32:10,059
And the mystery of how Titus even arrived in Spira is both interesting and related enough to the quest of

410
00:32:10,059 --> 00:32:13,880
the main party that they are also invested in discovering more about him.

411
00:32:14,579 --> 00:32:16,240
This is where Titus is meant to be.

412
00:32:16,819 --> 00:32:20,240
The answers to his mystery lay somewhere at the end of this journey.

413
00:32:21,019 --> 00:32:25,920
However, my favorite of these dynamics is Final Fantasy VIII's by far.

414
00:32:26,799 --> 00:32:30,619
What starts out simply enough as Squall's first mission as a leader,

415
00:32:31,119 --> 00:32:34,259
aiding a small resistance group from a city called Timber,

416
00:32:34,920 --> 00:33:05,579
quickly spot to the

417
00:33:05,579 --> 00:33:05,579
end of the story, and the end of the story.

418
00:33:05,579 --> 00:33:08,339
And the story of the story is that she can see you in the middle of a conspiracy to assassinate

419
00:33:08,339 --> 00:33:11,359
her before she can seize control of Galbadia's military.

420
00:33:12,599 --> 00:33:19,359
There is a constantly evolving reason why the characters are directly invested in the plot outside of just merely following orders.

421
00:33:20,299 --> 00:33:26,319
Slight tangent, but my favorite of these reasons is the previously mentioned mystery of the shared narcolepsy.

422
00:33:27,119 --> 00:33:31,099
Like many things in this game, if you haven't played it, then it's kind of hard to explain.

423
00:33:32,500 --> 00:33:38,059
Before your mission even starts, Squall and his team suddenly pass out on the train heading to Timber,

424
00:33:38,660 --> 00:33:44,359
whereby they have a shared dream about a group of Galbadian soldiers named Laguna, Kuros, and Ward,

425
00:33:44,839 --> 00:33:46,380
who are fighting in the Timber area.

426
00:33:47,380 --> 00:33:50,319
When the dream ends, they all realize they saw the same thing,

427
00:33:51,059 --> 00:33:53,420
but have no clue as to why it even happened at all.

428
00:33:54,299 --> 00:33:59,380
These visions continue to follow Squall and anyone who joins his team throughout the game,

429
00:33:59,799 --> 00:34:04,079
giving added context to some of the factions and locations you encounter in the game,

430
00:34:04,460 --> 00:34:08,719
as well as clearly demonstrating that there is something supernatural targeting their group.

431
00:34:09,460 --> 00:34:10,900
Laguna also just kicks ass.

432
00:34:11,400 --> 00:34:14,920
He's a fucking amazing character, he has a sick battle theme,

433
00:34:15,260 --> 00:34:19,059
and is probably the most regular and relatable dude in the entire franchise.

434
00:34:20,460 --> 00:34:23,960
I'm doing it again, but if this were an episode of a sitcom,

435
00:34:24,280 --> 00:34:26,659
these visions would serve as the B-plot.

436
00:34:27,119 --> 00:34:32,719
And while they don't really feel terribly related at first, they are interesting enough to string you along.

437
00:34:33,179 --> 00:34:37,639
And it also plants the seed in your head of about whether or not the party will ever meet up

438
00:34:37,639 --> 00:34:42,219
with Laguna in person, and this has a pretty neat payoff near the end of the story.

439
00:34:43,340 --> 00:34:48,579
With that tangent over, I want to talk more about Squall and his character arcs.

440
00:34:49,820 --> 00:34:51,260
I really like Squall as a protagonist.

441
00:34:52,320 --> 00:34:56,789
I can see more of myself in him than I do with most other video game protagonists.

442
00:34:57,679 --> 00:35:02,639
I hope that you are now picturing me as a brooding teenager who dresses in chains and dark clothing.

443
00:35:03,860 --> 00:35:05,599
What I will say is this.

444
00:35:06,300 --> 00:35:10,679
When creating a protagonist for your RPG, you have two choices that you can make.

445
00:35:11,420 --> 00:35:13,440
You can have your protagonist say mostly nothing.

446
00:35:14,159 --> 00:35:17,920
That way you can much more easily project yourself into the shoes of that character,

447
00:35:18,480 --> 00:35:25,579
as that character is never at risk of saying or doing anything that would otherwise interfere with your ability to see yourself as that character.

448
00:35:26,559 --> 00:35:29,519
You can safely imagine what those characters are saying in their head,

449
00:35:29,880 --> 00:35:33,400
without having to try and figure out what they might be like in person.

450
00:35:34,519 --> 00:35:40,280
Or you can go ahead and do the complete opposite thing and create a much more well-defined character.

451
00:35:41,000 --> 00:35:43,460
One who speaks and acts with their own agency.

452
00:35:44,039 --> 00:35:46,219
Which is a much riskier choice by far,

453
00:35:46,360 --> 00:35:53,420
as you can easily alienate players by having your protagonist say or do something that a player would not do in a given situation.

454
00:35:54,320 --> 00:36:01,239
However, this character has far more potential to embody the specific themes that the story may be trying to convey.

455
00:36:02,619 --> 00:36:04,760
Squall is clearly the latter of those two.

456
00:36:05,539 --> 00:36:09,539
You can narrow down his character pretty easily into two major arcs.

457
00:36:10,460 --> 00:36:13,019
The first is him embracing his role as a leader,

458
00:36:13,679 --> 00:36:16,480
and the second is his romantic relationship with Rinoa.

459
00:36:17,940 --> 00:36:20,039
It's important to keep these two arcs in mind,

460
00:36:20,360 --> 00:36:24,960
as the squall we meet at the start of the game hardly seems capable of being a leader.

461
00:36:25,420 --> 00:36:28,579
In fact, he starts out as a student, a follower.

462
00:36:29,880 --> 00:36:33,719
He is also shown to not really be interested in romance of any kind.

463
00:36:34,599 --> 00:36:40,340
He is highly introverted, has inner monologues, and seems bothered to even have to deal with other people.

464
00:36:41,320 --> 00:36:45,860
He is also surrounded by people who seem to understand him better than he understands himself.

465
00:36:46,579 --> 00:36:50,679
And are essentially able to trick him into caring about things he otherwise wouldn't.

466
00:36:51,679 --> 00:36:53,119
He doesn't know who he is yet.

467
00:36:54,059 --> 00:36:59,719
The only reason he dances with Rinoa at the start of the game is because of how forceful her personality is.

468
00:37:00,340 --> 00:37:02,679
She is essentially a foil to him in this scene.

469
00:37:03,780 --> 00:37:06,179
This introduction is important, because again,

470
00:37:06,719 --> 00:37:11,420
the squall we see at the end of the game hardly seems like the character we met at the beginning.

471
00:37:12,440 --> 00:37:16,800
The options to choose dialogue that are outright dismissive, the ellipses whatever,

472
00:37:17,519 --> 00:37:23,019
are slowly phased out of the game as squall is thrust into situations where he is forced to be a leader.

473
00:37:24,059 --> 00:37:27,860
I think it is also important to distinguish that squall doesn't want to be a leader.

474
00:37:28,360 --> 00:37:29,539
He is being made into one.

475
00:37:30,280 --> 00:37:31,980
He is just a seed, if you will.

476
00:37:32,659 --> 00:37:35,639
Planted into the role of a leader, whether he likes it or not,

477
00:37:36,039 --> 00:37:39,539
and whether he can grow into a successful leader is up to him.

478
00:37:40,300 --> 00:37:43,139
His decision is whether or not to embrace that role.

479
00:37:44,159 --> 00:37:47,079
The internal monologue he has is useful to the player,

480
00:37:47,300 --> 00:37:51,980
as it lets you see that the character isn't rapidly changing into something that he wasn't before.

481
00:37:52,579 --> 00:37:55,380
However, that isn't what everyone else sees.

482
00:37:56,019 --> 00:38:01,079
They can only see squall, the most capable person in any given situation.

483
00:38:01,960 --> 00:38:05,880
He is the character that is most fit to be a leader among all others,

484
00:38:06,380 --> 00:38:10,300
as we see time and time again demonstrations of failed leadership.

485
00:38:11,460 --> 00:38:15,119
We see Cypher, who is too cavalier to even follow basic orders.

486
00:38:16,099 --> 00:38:20,059
Quistis, who fails to even maintain order with her own students.

487
00:38:20,679 --> 00:38:28,599
And even Headmaster Cid, who eventually buckles under the weight of a crisis and has to rely on squall to save Balambgarden.

488
00:38:29,619 --> 00:38:31,300
This is why I like squall.

489
00:38:31,940 --> 00:38:37,179
Not because I'm edgy, but because I can empathize with him far more than I can with other protagonists.

490
00:38:37,909 --> 00:38:42,880
We have all been thrust into a situation in which we have felt that we weren't in control anymore.

491
00:38:43,519 --> 00:38:49,719
And watching squall struggle, and then eventually succeed over his own problems, makes him more heroic and relatable.

492
00:38:50,480 --> 00:38:52,360
Someone that we want to aspire to be.

493
00:38:53,079 --> 00:38:53,679
A leader.

494
00:38:55,480 --> 00:39:00,480
Squall's second arc might even be more important to the story, depending on your interpretation of events.

495
00:39:01,699 --> 00:39:07,239
One thing to point out is that while squall does eventually become more confident in himself as a leader,

496
00:39:07,719 --> 00:39:11,659
eventually learning to rely on others and not to try and solve everything himself,

497
00:39:12,460 --> 00:39:18,440
at some point his lack of confidence returns, and it's when he starts to become more attached to Rinoa.

498
00:39:20,860 --> 00:39:23,719
I want to clarify that I do not like love stories.

499
00:39:24,059 --> 00:39:25,159
I never have.

500
00:39:25,679 --> 00:39:28,039
And I don't even have a great reason as to why.

501
00:39:29,199 --> 00:39:31,099
I won't imply that I hate them,

502
00:39:31,360 --> 00:39:35,699
but I can't name a single romance film that I like off the top of my head,

503
00:39:36,239 --> 00:39:39,920
and in books or games that feature it, it's usually my least favorite part.

504
00:39:41,059 --> 00:39:45,300
It's probably because a lot of romance comes off as forced or unnatural,

505
00:39:45,920 --> 00:39:49,880
or at worst, it's just sexual attraction between two good-looking characters.

506
00:39:51,300 --> 00:39:54,280
For me, real love does not look like it does in the movies.

507
00:39:54,280 --> 00:39:55,840
It's far more complicated.

508
00:39:56,500 --> 00:40:02,280
I could probably count the number of love stories that I genuinely enjoy using one half of one hand,

509
00:40:02,880 --> 00:40:05,059
and Final Fantasy VIII is one of those exceptions.

510
00:40:06,500 --> 00:40:10,480
I've read people's criticisms of the love story, saying that it also feels forced,

511
00:40:10,820 --> 00:40:12,179
but I can't agree with that.

512
00:40:13,260 --> 00:40:16,900
It does have this scene with a very sappy love song,

513
00:40:17,239 --> 00:40:19,059
but it's not like we're seeing the two get married.

514
00:40:20,000 --> 00:40:25,400
The climax of their love story is essentially the two of them realizing that they do actually love each other.

515
00:40:26,099 --> 00:40:27,139
But it's complicated.

516
00:40:28,139 --> 00:40:35,199
The song itself is an in-universe reference to Laguna and Julia, Squall's maybe dad and Rinoa's definitely mom,

517
00:40:35,480 --> 00:40:37,079
who we know did not end up together.

518
00:40:38,440 --> 00:40:41,739
This scene is filled with apprehension about what comes next,

519
00:40:42,360 --> 00:40:49,219
and more importantly, how we got here feels pretty natural, especially considering the very fantastical circumstances.

520
00:40:50,440 --> 00:40:54,440
It's not as though there wasn't 30 hours of bumbling from these two dummies before we got here.

521
00:40:55,679 --> 00:41:01,579
Whether you like this or not, this is undoubtedly the legacy that Final Fantasy VIII left behind.

522
00:41:02,179 --> 00:41:04,239
Well, this and Triple Triad.

523
00:41:05,260 --> 00:41:11,119
Final Fantasy IX and X would go on to focus more heavily on a romantic subplot as a major part

524
00:41:11,119 --> 00:41:16,739
of its story, with X even channeling the sappy love song energy in one of its most famous scenes.

525
00:41:20,820 --> 00:41:23,519
For my money, though, the romance worked best in VIII,

526
00:41:23,880 --> 00:41:26,809
as it is clearly the focus of one of the game's major themes.

527
00:41:28,199 --> 00:41:33,780
This game features a TON of love stories, mostly failed ones that end in tragedy.

528
00:41:34,800 --> 00:41:37,940
There is Quistus' unrequited love for Squall, as I mentioned,

529
00:41:38,760 --> 00:41:42,500
which pays off in a great scene that I have to mention because it's one of my favorites.

530
00:41:43,489 --> 00:41:46,539
Right after Rinoa and Squall return from space,

531
00:41:46,960 --> 00:41:51,500
and Squall fails to convince Rinoa not to put herself in stasis for everyone's protection,

532
00:41:52,159 --> 00:41:57,360
Quistus then has to remind the dummy why he even jumped into space to save her in the first place,

533
00:41:57,699 --> 00:41:59,579
in which Squall basically goes, Die?

534
00:42:01,159 --> 00:42:04,460
It's one of the most sobering and adult moments in the franchise,

535
00:42:04,820 --> 00:42:09,820
and it has the subtext of Quistus saying, You fucking broke my heart, you piece of shit.

536
00:42:09,820 --> 00:42:10,980
Now go save your girlfriend.

537
00:42:12,219 --> 00:42:16,300
Rinoa herself had a crush on Cipher at the start, if you can remember,

538
00:42:16,960 --> 00:42:22,179
which she admits to after it is revealed that Cipher was executed for his attack on the president.

539
00:42:22,519 --> 00:42:28,500
This, of course, also comes after she admitted that the only reason she danced with Squall at the beginning was

540
00:42:28,500 --> 00:42:32,760
because she was looking for someone, although there was probably something else there too.

541
00:42:33,780 --> 00:42:36,760
Later on, we also learn that Edia and Cid were married,

542
00:42:37,000 --> 00:42:41,079
which sort of recontextualizes that whole sorceress assassination thing that Cid had.

543
00:42:42,400 --> 00:42:47,460
And of course, there is the horrific story that is Laguna's love life, which I won't even get into.

544
00:42:48,559 --> 00:42:52,920
When you establish a setting in which love almost invariably leads to tragedy,

545
00:42:53,539 --> 00:42:57,699
it makes the one that might actually work out feel all the more special,

546
00:42:58,119 --> 00:42:59,380
and worth fighting for.

547
00:43:00,159 --> 00:43:03,539
It's a strong motivator to continue the story, to say the least.

548
00:43:03,639 --> 00:43:07,900
Alright, this section has already grown into a monster,

549
00:43:08,659 --> 00:43:11,320
but I still gotta address the elephant in the room,

550
00:43:11,539 --> 00:43:15,960
which is all that shit that happens when this asshole starts shooting baskets,

551
00:43:16,199 --> 00:43:19,219
and all that shit that happens right at the start of Disc 3.

552
00:43:20,119 --> 00:43:22,019
Some of you may have seen this coming.

553
00:43:22,659 --> 00:43:26,239
I don't want to further bog things down by explaining the entire story,

554
00:43:26,400 --> 00:43:31,840
but allow me to briefly describe the two major plot twists in the game for those who don't know.

555
00:43:33,960 --> 00:43:39,639
So Disc 1 concludes with a battle against the sorceress Edia, whom you have been tasked with assassinating

556
00:43:39,980 --> 00:43:42,300
before she can take over all of Galbadia.

557
00:43:43,320 --> 00:43:48,659
Despite winning the battle, the fight concludes with you failing to kill Edia, catching an icicle,

558
00:43:48,840 --> 00:43:51,679
and then waking up in prison at the start of Disc 2.

559
00:43:52,760 --> 00:43:57,000
It seems at this point that they are setting up Edia to become the ultimate villain of the game,

560
00:43:57,440 --> 00:44:02,739
which would seem pretty obvious considering the amount the game and the promotional material focuses on her.

561
00:44:03,820 --> 00:44:08,780
So then, you might be surprised to hear that the game actually tries to step away from this idea,

562
00:44:09,719 --> 00:44:15,900
and that all starts right here with this infamous basketball scene, the first major twist of the game.

563
00:44:16,960 --> 00:44:22,460
It is revealed by Irvine why earlier in the game he had hesitated to take his shot against Edia,

564
00:44:22,860 --> 00:44:25,960
which could have killed her without the need to engage her in a fight.

565
00:44:27,119 --> 00:44:31,800
What originally just seemed like nerves in the first moment of Irvine's character development,

566
00:44:32,480 --> 00:44:38,960
he instead reveals that he couldn't shoot her because she is the woman who took care of them all when they were children.

567
00:44:39,860 --> 00:44:43,380
You see, the entire cast of the game, minus Rinoa,

568
00:44:43,500 --> 00:44:49,420
but including Cipher and another character I won't even get into, were all orphans who stayed at the same orphanage,

569
00:44:49,840 --> 00:44:51,519
and Edia was their caretaker.

570
00:44:52,599 --> 00:44:57,280
It is revealed that every single character has amnesia, including Edia,

571
00:44:57,860 --> 00:45:03,400
and the reason for this is likely due to the fact that members of Seed draw their powers from Guardian

572
00:45:03,400 --> 00:45:06,239
Forces, who in turn take their memories.

573
00:45:07,400 --> 00:45:13,559
The reason Irvine remembers this is because he had never used Guardian Forces until he joined up with your team.

574
00:45:14,840 --> 00:45:21,300
I believe the purpose of this bombshell of information is to make you question the nature of Guardian Forces,

575
00:45:21,559 --> 00:45:22,420
and sorceresses.

576
00:45:23,480 --> 00:45:28,400
How does a demonstrably caring woman come to nearly kill the children she raised?

577
00:45:28,840 --> 00:45:32,900
And how do those children almost assassinate their surrogate mother without realizing it?

578
00:45:33,840 --> 00:45:36,800
There is something clearly unnatural at play here.

579
00:45:37,280 --> 00:45:38,219
A new mystery.

580
00:45:39,559 --> 00:45:44,679
Most people get caught up on how incredibly convenient it is that none of the characters remembered each other.

581
00:45:45,260 --> 00:45:50,420
But what this scene does do is alter the characters' motivation and plan of attack moving forward.

582
00:45:51,239 --> 00:45:53,510
They no longer want to kill the sorceress.

583
00:45:54,039 --> 00:45:55,989
They would save her if it is at all possible.

584
00:45:57,280 --> 00:46:00,639
The beginning of disc 3 then is the aftermath to this setup,

585
00:46:01,179 --> 00:46:05,099
where after you finally defeat Edia in your rematch at the end of disc 2,

586
00:46:05,980 --> 00:46:09,039
the game pulls the trigger and has her regain her memories,

587
00:46:09,699 --> 00:46:12,519
or in this case, control over her own body.

588
00:46:13,380 --> 00:46:18,019
She reveals, halfway through the game, that all of her actions were not her own,

589
00:46:18,480 --> 00:46:22,840
but she was actually being controlled by another character that we haven't even heard of yet,

590
00:46:23,639 --> 00:46:26,389
the sorceress living in the future, Ultimecia.

591
00:46:27,000 --> 00:46:31,039
Again, another twist that feels almost completely out of left field,

592
00:46:31,360 --> 00:46:36,380
and made all the more unbelievable considering the game's hyper-focus on Edia up to this point.

593
00:46:37,400 --> 00:46:41,719
I would be lying if I said that when I got to this point on my original playthrough that I

594
00:46:41,719 --> 00:46:46,360
wasn't also scratching my head, wondering what the hell this game was even trying to do.

595
00:46:47,460 --> 00:46:51,119
At this point in the story, there are so many unanswered questions,

596
00:46:51,699 --> 00:46:54,699
and those that get addressed are given very confusing answers.

597
00:46:55,860 --> 00:46:59,679
A lot of people that I knew who played this game mentally checked out at this point,

598
00:47:00,059 --> 00:47:03,619
or just straight up quit playing, and it's not really hard to see why.

599
00:47:04,380 --> 00:47:08,059
And while I recognize that this all could have been handled a lot better,

600
00:47:08,659 --> 00:47:11,699
I do appreciate what disc 3 was trying to do.

601
00:47:12,539 --> 00:47:17,500
It tries to recontextualize the initial threat of the game as being just a symptom of a much,

602
00:47:18,099 --> 00:47:20,760
much greater threat that you aren't even sure how to face yet.

603
00:47:21,860 --> 00:47:23,039
For those who stuck with it,

604
00:47:23,500 --> 00:47:26,539
this is the part of the game that gives you the bulk of the world building,

605
00:47:26,880 --> 00:47:30,800
and it goes on to inform us how sorceresses and magic operate in this setting.

606
00:47:31,619 --> 00:47:36,780
It also does actually resolve most of the plot threads, and gives us a conclusion to Laguna's story.

607
00:47:37,940 --> 00:47:42,179
If you made it to disc 3, then I would definitely suggest sticking around to the end,

608
00:47:42,300 --> 00:47:44,280
as there is some crazy shit that happens.

609
00:47:45,300 --> 00:47:49,440
And by the time that disc 4 starts, there are very few unanswered questions,

610
00:47:50,019 --> 00:47:53,480
with the last major one being the true motivations of the villain.

611
00:47:54,340 --> 00:47:59,400
And that is where I get incredibly controversial and start to talk about a fan theory.

612
00:48:00,099 --> 00:48:03,159
The Rinoa is Ultimecia theory, if you are aware of it.

613
00:48:03,800 --> 00:48:05,440
Oh boy, get excited.

614
00:48:07,219 --> 00:48:09,219
So this is going to be the king of all tangents.

615
00:48:09,860 --> 00:48:14,420
I know that fan theories aren't for everyone, and that this isn't an Iceberg Explained video,

616
00:48:15,099 --> 00:48:19,000
but this theory covers the version of events that I interpret when I play the game,

617
00:48:19,539 --> 00:48:22,699
and I promise that there is actually a point to all of this in the end.

618
00:48:23,500 --> 00:48:25,699
Anyways, I guess consider this as a warning.

619
00:48:25,980 --> 00:48:29,000
If you are still here, then here we fucking go.

620
00:48:30,260 --> 00:48:35,099
The Rinoa is Ultimecia theory is one of the few fan theories that I wholly subscribe to.

621
00:48:36,000 --> 00:48:38,159
I usually dismiss fan theories offhandedly,

622
00:48:38,480 --> 00:48:43,340
as they typically require me to make far too many assumptions about things that aren't actually in the game,

623
00:48:43,820 --> 00:48:48,019
or they disregard whole swaths of it entirely, which is just not very interesting to me.

624
00:48:49,079 --> 00:48:55,980
I make an exception here because this theory actually fits in more details than the actual canonical English translation of this story.

625
00:48:57,039 --> 00:49:00,900
Ultimecia does not get a lot of screen time in Final Fantasy VIII,

626
00:49:01,280 --> 00:49:04,300
but you do actually learn about what she is attempting to accomplish.

627
00:49:05,260 --> 00:49:08,619
She lives in a bleak, war-torn, and apocalyptic future,

628
00:49:09,260 --> 00:49:12,760
and her goal is to complete something known as the Compression of Time.

629
00:49:13,679 --> 00:49:18,059
It is explained that this would combine the past, present, and future into a theme.

630
00:49:19,320 --> 00:49:24,860
The canonical reason for this is because Ultimecia is attempting to prevent her own downfall,

631
00:49:25,300 --> 00:49:29,300
as there is a destiny about a legendary seed that will come to defeat her,

632
00:49:29,760 --> 00:49:34,440
and so she seeks to prevent this fate from ever happening via a timeline loophole.

633
00:49:35,239 --> 00:49:37,920
Unfortunately, that's all you really get with Ultimecia.

634
00:49:38,800 --> 00:49:43,380
You can assume her controlling Edea was an attempt to have her destroy seed altogether,

635
00:49:44,199 --> 00:49:46,800
but in the end she fails, and paradoxically,

636
00:49:47,199 --> 00:49:50,719
she ends up traveling back to bestow her sorceress powers to her sorceress to her sorceress powers to Edea in

637
00:49:50,719 --> 00:49:54,039
the past, setting off the events of the story to begin with.

638
00:49:55,440 --> 00:50:00,059
It's explained that all sorceresses need to pass on their power before they can die,

639
00:50:00,840 --> 00:50:03,360
and even if you take this statement at face value,

640
00:50:03,980 --> 00:50:08,960
it was made clear earlier that only specific women can inherit the power of a sorceress.

641
00:50:09,920 --> 00:50:13,139
If the big bad guy isn't Edea, or even Adele,

642
00:50:13,460 --> 00:50:18,119
because they weren't around in the future, it stands to question, who the hell is Ultimecia?

643
00:50:18,480 --> 00:50:22,380
Well, if you go through the game a second time,

644
00:50:22,679 --> 00:50:25,480
with the idea that Rinoa might actually be Ultimecia,

645
00:50:25,980 --> 00:50:30,239
certain details of the story start to line up which otherwise have no reason to be there.

646
00:50:31,900 --> 00:50:36,280
So Squall wears this ring with a lion design on it, much like many of his accessories.

647
00:50:37,500 --> 00:50:43,519
There is a point in the game in which Zell tricks Squall into giving him this ring because Rinoa asked him to.

648
00:50:44,280 --> 00:50:45,340
She just seems to like the ring.

649
00:50:46,639 --> 00:50:49,639
For whatever reason, during the middle of a pitched battle,

650
00:50:50,000 --> 00:50:53,900
Rinoa gives the ring back to him and asks Squall if it has a name.

651
00:50:54,860 --> 00:50:57,360
By default, this ring's name is Griever,

652
00:50:57,659 --> 00:51:00,699
which Squall gives back to her and tells her that she can keep it.

653
00:51:01,860 --> 00:51:07,639
Enter Ultimecia, who during her final battle decides to summon a guardian force of all things,

654
00:51:08,300 --> 00:51:13,039
which doesn't really make a whole lot of sense to me as to why a sorceress would even need a

655
00:51:13,039 --> 00:51:15,440
guardian force if they can use magic inherently.

656
00:51:16,280 --> 00:51:18,820
They are the only humans that can do this in the lore,

657
00:51:19,239 --> 00:51:24,639
so it seems very strange for the developers to add this detail into the final fight if it meant nothing.

658
00:51:25,440 --> 00:51:28,079
But also, it clearly means something,

659
00:51:28,659 --> 00:51:34,820
as the guardian force she summons is a lion creature that has the exact same name as whatever Squall decides

660
00:51:34,820 --> 00:51:37,400
to name his ring, or Griever by default.

661
00:51:38,559 --> 00:51:43,199
This is why this theory was born, as this whole thing begs further examination,

662
00:51:43,760 --> 00:51:46,920
and writing it all off as a coincidence is almost silly.

663
00:51:47,840 --> 00:51:52,019
There is a connection between Squall and Ultimecia, it's just not well explained.

664
00:51:53,159 --> 00:51:58,139
The basic theory states this then, that if Ultimecia was using a guardian force,

665
00:51:58,659 --> 00:52:03,000
would she not also be affected by the amnesia that affects everyone else who uses them?

666
00:52:03,920 --> 00:52:09,460
If so, is it possible that Ultimecia forgot the real reason why she wanted to compress time,

667
00:52:09,940 --> 00:52:13,219
confusing it with a paranoid destiny about her eventual destruction?

668
00:52:14,400 --> 00:52:16,909
If you choose to believe that Rinoa is Ultimecia,

669
00:52:17,219 --> 00:52:21,420
then you have a pretty good answer as to where she first heard about this destiny of hers,

670
00:52:21,739 --> 00:52:26,739
as this was a nightmare that Rinoa described to Squall just before they set out on their final mission.

671
00:52:27,960 --> 00:52:31,260
This turns Ultimecia into a very tragic figure,

672
00:52:31,900 --> 00:52:35,800
a woman who inherited powers she didn't ask for and can't even control,

673
00:52:36,820 --> 00:52:39,739
powers that are feared by every single person in the world.

674
00:52:40,900 --> 00:52:45,199
If she used guardian forces, she would eventually forget what was important to her,

675
00:52:45,760 --> 00:52:48,559
potentially even being forced to fight against her friends.

676
00:52:49,840 --> 00:52:52,719
After years of fighting against the entire world,

677
00:52:53,360 --> 00:52:57,119
all that she would have to remember those people by would be physical mementos,

678
00:52:57,820 --> 00:53:02,900
small inconsequential things that no longer carried the greater context of why they mattered at all.

679
00:53:03,840 --> 00:53:06,900
That could very well be her motivation to compress time.

680
00:53:07,760 --> 00:53:13,219
It would allow her to escape her destroyed future and let her live in a single moment that she managed

681
00:53:13,219 --> 00:53:17,159
to cling onto, a moment of greater significance than the rest.

682
00:53:18,320 --> 00:53:21,860
While Rinoa would want to return to a world where Squall still existed,

683
00:53:22,480 --> 00:53:27,559
Ultimecia would only remember the need to compress time into a single moment to escape her fate.

684
00:53:28,500 --> 00:53:31,460
She cannot remember the motivation, just the ambition.

685
00:53:32,659 --> 00:53:37,800
This consequently creates a reality in which she is slain by the very person she was attempting to see,

686
00:53:38,480 --> 00:53:41,840
which might have actually been the ending she was looking for from the start.

687
00:53:42,860 --> 00:53:47,340
Honestly, reading some of the dialogue near the end of the game makes this theory almost seem a little on

688
00:53:47,340 --> 00:53:51,139
the nose, but I also realize that confirmation bias exists.

689
00:53:52,179 --> 00:53:57,320
The canonical Ultimecia is the story of a woman who desperately wanted to change destiny,

690
00:53:57,840 --> 00:54:02,159
only to paradoxically set forth a series of events that would eventually lead to that destiny.

691
00:54:03,460 --> 00:54:05,559
It fits well within the themes of the game,

692
00:54:06,019 --> 00:54:08,760
but I like the theory's version a lot better.

693
00:54:09,820 --> 00:54:13,019
The game leaves off with the idea that love transcends all,

694
00:54:13,719 --> 00:54:20,400
as it is heavily implied that Squall and Rinoa's promise to each other is what allows her to find and rescue Squall in the end.

695
00:54:21,539 --> 00:54:25,420
The risk you take in loving someone is the grief you feel when you lose them,

696
00:54:25,960 --> 00:54:30,500
and so a grief-stricken Rinoa, who has been transformed into a monster of sorts,

697
00:54:30,840 --> 00:54:33,119
makes sense as the villain of this story.

698
00:54:34,639 --> 00:54:38,619
I want to double down and state that I know that this isn't actually part of the game,

699
00:54:39,099 --> 00:54:43,480
and that this theory was debunked by one of the writers slash director for the game.

700
00:54:44,179 --> 00:54:45,840
However, I don't care.

701
00:54:46,840 --> 00:54:48,059
Not to throw shade at the guy,

702
00:54:48,239 --> 00:54:52,090
but if we took the writers' word as gospel when it came to their own creations,

703
00:54:52,860 --> 00:54:57,480
then the wizards at Hogwarts would be shitting on the floor, or whatever other crazy shit that J.K.

704
00:54:57,619 --> 00:55:01,000
Rowling has said, and Deckard from Blade Runner would be a replicant.

705
00:55:02,340 --> 00:55:06,800
It's not the writer's job to tell you how to interpret a story after it has been released.

706
00:55:07,659 --> 00:55:10,019
When you write your story to have a mystery,

707
00:55:10,300 --> 00:55:13,780
or imply that there is greater depth to something even though there really isn't,

708
00:55:14,559 --> 00:55:18,730
you allow for the person experiencing your story to fill in the blanks with their own interpretation,

709
00:55:19,380 --> 00:55:21,980
who then creates a personal connection to your story.

710
00:55:23,179 --> 00:55:26,179
A well-made book or game will contain many of these,

711
00:55:26,760 --> 00:55:32,019
and it's why you see so many fan theories for well-liked franchises that have incredibly loyal fanbases.

712
00:55:32,739 --> 00:55:34,460
Think Game of Thrones or Dark Souls.

713
00:55:35,679 --> 00:55:40,659
In the same way that some people interpret Squall to be a brooding, over-emotional brat,

714
00:55:41,360 --> 00:55:44,659
I see an introverted teenager with a lot on his mind.

715
00:55:45,360 --> 00:55:47,280
Neither interpretation is incorrect.

716
00:55:48,780 --> 00:55:55,309
To me, Rinoa is Ultimecia, because as a concept for a villain, I find her to be incredibly interesting,

717
00:55:55,880 --> 00:56:00,000
and I would love for someone in the future to create a character based on that concept.

718
00:56:00,860 --> 00:56:04,179
And so, despite the narrative, or even the game,

719
00:56:04,400 --> 00:56:08,409
of Final Fantasy VIII not really being as tightly put together as some of its contemporaries,

720
00:56:09,380 --> 00:56:16,139
there are still enough small details and moments of genuine quality in this game that help me to overlook some of its weaker elements.

721
00:56:16,780 --> 00:56:18,519
And each time that I play this game,

722
00:56:18,760 --> 00:56:23,070
I find myself discovering more things that I had somehow missed on previous playthroughs.

723
00:56:24,320 --> 00:56:25,579
For those wondering, by the way,

724
00:56:25,840 --> 00:56:31,320
there are mods out there that people have come up with that try to solve the gameplay and balance issues.

725
00:56:32,139 --> 00:56:36,820
I myself have always thought about what I would do if given the chance to tweak the systems of the

726
00:56:36,820 --> 00:56:39,079
game, or flesh out some of the ideas in the story.

727
00:56:40,099 --> 00:56:40,699
But you know what?

728
00:56:41,400 --> 00:56:45,280
For nostalgia's sake, even if given that opportunity, I would probably stick to the original.

729
00:56:47,750 --> 00:56:53,400
Anyways, to quickly summarize, Final Fantasy VIII is better than VII because it has Laguna.

730
00:56:54,019 --> 00:56:54,400
Bye-bye!

731
00:56:57,000 --> 00:57:02,539
Alright, I hope you found at least some of what I talked about here to be interesting, entertaining,

732
00:57:02,760 --> 00:57:03,849
or at the least informative.

733
00:57:05,239 --> 00:57:10,199
My long-winded rant is officially over, and if you've managed to make it this far through the entire thing,

734
00:57:10,760 --> 00:57:12,099
then I cannot thank you enough.

735
00:57:13,000 --> 00:57:14,280
Please leave your comments or criticisms if you have any questions.

736
00:57:16,139 --> 00:57:20,400
This was a very different type of video than what I was used to making,

737
00:57:20,679 --> 00:57:24,179
and it is much more in line with me ranting and raving at my friends

738
00:57:24,599 --> 00:57:26,960
about some obscure subject that they don't care about at all.

739
00:57:27,699 --> 00:57:30,400
This really is the iconic Sean Cece rant.

740
00:57:31,500 --> 00:57:33,840
I tried my best to make this a shorter video,

741
00:57:34,099 --> 00:57:39,739
but after writing and rewriting it multiple times, it kept changing into something else entirely.

742
00:57:40,139 --> 00:57:43,280
As it turns out, I had a lot more to say about Final Fantasy VIII than I thought I'd be doing.

743
00:57:45,579 --> 00:57:51,320
I'm really scared that I might learn that maybe I'm just not capable of making shorter videos, but who knows.

744
00:57:52,619 --> 00:57:56,019
Thank you again for watching, and there are more videos to come.

745
00:57:57,340 --> 00:57:58,079
I love you.

746
00:57:59,179 --> 00:57:59,280
Bye-bye!

